(*
Issue #204: a flat chain of `1+1+1+...+1` used as an ARRAY BOUND crashes the
compiler even though the identical chain in an ordinary statement does not
(see deeply-chained-addition-does-not-crash-the-compiler.pas next door,
which is exactly that statement-context case).  resolveTypeImpl
(SemaType.cpp) calls checkExpr on a bound first, which correctly hits
Sema::MaxExprDepth (Sema.h) and reports "nested too deeply" -- the crash is
not there.  foldBounds then walks the SAME chain a second time through
constBound/constBoundImpl (and, for a schema array body, buildExtentForm,
which shares the exposure by recursing into itself the same way) with no
depth guard at all, and it is that second, unguarded walk which actually
exhausts the C++ stack and segfaults, moments after the first walk already
diagnosed the very same expression cleanly.

The fix gives constBound and buildExtentForm the same ExprDepth counter and
MaxExprDepth ceiling checkExpr already uses, rather than a second,
independent limit of their own (both now declared on Sema, Sema.h, so
SemaType.cpp can reach them too) -- constBoundImpl now "share[s] the same
depth budget as checkExpr", exactly as the issue asked for.  Checked in
verbatim rather than generated at build time, matching
deeply-chained-addition-does-not-crash-the-compiler.pas's own precedent --
lit has no loop-emitting primitive, and the source is deterministic; nobody
should have to look at 200000 terms to read this file, so the lit
directives come first.
*)

(*
RUN: not %plang %s -o %t 2> %t.err
RUN: FileCheck %s < %t.err
*)

(*
CHECK: nested too deeply
*)

program t;
var a: array[1..] of char;
begin end.
